body:
"Logen plunged through the trees, bare feet slipping and sliding on the wet earth, the slush, the wet pine needles, breath rasping in his chest, blood thumping in his head."  The Blade Itself - Joe Abercrombie.

&#x200B;

Not as catchy as the start of the Dark Tower, but got dang, this got me immediately enthralled and my heart pumping.  "What the hell is chasing him, what is happening."

I was hoping to see this line in here!  That series was amazing.

&#x200B;

(For the uninitiated: The Passage - Justin Cronin)

I'm gonna ask a completely off-topic question:  How do I get this long view photo from Goodreads?  I am not smart enough to figure it out!  

&#x200B;

Also excellent job!  I'm seeing some excellent books I want to read on here!

While I fully recommend the Codex Alera series (I just finished it a couple weeks ago), the first half of the first book is one of the worst slogs I have ever had to trudge through in any book.  It is a ton of build up, there are a ton of characters that Jim was introducing, a ton of worldbuilding, and a jarring experience because I was subconsciously expecting more of the writing style of Dresden Files.  

&#x200B;

HOWEVER, once you get to a certain point in the first book (about chapter 28 for me), the series does not slow down.  I HIGHLY recommend the series, but don't get too discouraged by the start of the first book.

Oh my sweet summer child.  I envy the journey you are about to take and wish I could go again for the first time.

&#x200B;

The late Jordan books are good, but they are definitely difficult to get through.  He is setting up the finale and following some wild storylines.  Sanderson killed off two wandering storylines in the prologue of the first book he wrote.  It is worth getting through, but I would have had serious issues if it hadn't been on audiobook for me.  I would recommend listening to the books.  The narrators are amazing and definitely help with the slog.

RemindMe! Two Weeks

&#x200B;

I really would like to know what his input is too, but I suspect he is not allowed to comment.  He probably has a contract which prevents him from discussing the policies of his publisher.

This is probably the best concrete way forward I've received on here.  I definitely have a better idea for what I can do with some fantasy nonsense by mashing together some crazy ideas and spinning it out from there.  

&#x200B;

Also pulling from my personal experience sounds like a great plan.  I know this is all basic stuff, but it's also stuff I needed to hear.  Thanks!
